A||||7||OzFiero||Wheel advice||||5||
Z||000000||OzFiero||11-05-2000||06:48 PM||||I want to buy some after market 17" wheels for my '88 Formula. The stock rear wheels are 1" wider than the front which I guess is necessary for the suspension to work the way it was designed but makes buying after market wheels expensive as the package deals come in sets of 4. Does anyone know the downside of fitting the same width all around on a stock suspension ?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cheever2||11-05-2000||08:43 PM||||Go for it.  Not a problem.  <BR>Might want to play with the search function here and look for "wheel sizes" etc.  If still don't have your answers, put another reply here and you're sure to get some great advice real soon.<BR>Welcome to da Forum!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||OzFiero||11-06-2000||12:20 AM||||I did the search thing but couldn't find the reason for the difference in wheel width. I'm sure it must have been done as a part of the '88 suspension design. Seems common with mid engine cars to have wider wheel at the rear. I was just wondering if anyone understood why and what effect going from 6" to 7" in front and staying at 7" in the rear might have on the handling. ??||||reg||5
Z||000003||Cheever2||11-06-2000||01:04 AM||||There was a lot of discussion about wheel & tire sizes here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004917.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004917.html</A> <BR>Don't know if anybody can tell you WHY Pontiac made the rears wider, but I'm almost certain you can put same size all around if you want to without negative effects.  Keep looking here, and click the email box of some of these people who seem more intelligent than myself (shouldn't take long).  Usually the responses are quick.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||11-06-2000||02:33 AM||||Okay, here goes.<BR>Look at the high performance cars with mid or rear mounted engines.  They all have wider tires in the back.  To put it in lay terms.. Ever notice how if you come into a corner real hot in a Fiero and drop the throttle, the back end kicks out and tries to pass you?  This is because most of the wieght is in the back, and that weight has more momentum than the rest of the car.  Wider tires in the back help prevent drastic dropped throttle oversteer.  <P> I am not up on the technical aspects of the '88 front suspension, but I know the smaller front rims were part of the comprimise of a tighter turning radius. And I think the offset on the '88s is like 9mm more, right?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||11-06-2000||08:45 AM||||Having the same width all around in an 88 is OK. I personaly like the look of having wider rims in the back. Also thiner tires in front will make steering easier (less effort). If you don't care about the look buy them all equal and take opportunity of the offer.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||11-06-2000||11:40 AM||||I've got 225s on all 4 corners of my '88. Never felt any push, and only on the curviest of roads did I feel the back end try to sway. disadvantage is that it is hard as heck to try to steer those front wheels when you're not moving.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||11-06-2000||12:54 PM||||I knew a guy with an 88 GT who put matching widths front and back. The front tires stuck out on the front about an extra inch and a half. Looked horrible. My opinion only.||||reg||1
